  3. [1mNotifications[0m — change events, per-user fan-out, delivery, read state.

  Sub-projects 2 and 3 may end up merged; I'll know once 1 is settled. Each gets its own spec → plan → implementation
  cycle. Starting on sub-project 1.

  [1mThe fork for tasks:[0m whether the app has a server. This is the decision everything else inherits, and it's the least
  reversible one on the list.

  - [1mBrowser-only (localStorage)[0m — no backend, no accounts, no deploy story. Buys a working app today. But "users" is
    then just one person per browser, and notifications degrade to in-page toasts: there is no one to fan out to, and
    nothing can notify you while the tab is closed. Choosing this substantially narrows what sub-project 3 can be.
  - [1mServer + shared store[0m — real accounts, real multi-user tasks, and notifications that can reach someone who isn't
    looking at the page (email, push, or a notification feed they check later). Costs a backend, a database, and auth
    before any notification exists. Expensive to retrofit onto a localStorage app; cheap to build toward from the start.
  - [1mLocal-first with sync later[0m — build against a storage interface now, swap the implementation for a server later.
    Buys optionality, costs an abstraction layer you may never cash in, and doesn't actually defer the multi-user
    data-model questions — it just hides them.

  My recommendation is [1mserver + shared store[0m, because your original request already implies it. "Users" is plural and
  "tasks they care about" implies tasks that someone [3melse[0m can change — both require a shared store and a notion of
  identity. Building browser-only first would mean rewriting the task layer before notifications could do what you asked
  for.
